all have proper format, we can assume all lines are parseable. So we will parse each line as \"<type>: <content>\". If it fails, we treat as invalid and set error_line; for trajectory, we can still add an object with type being the full line? Not ideal. But we'll assume no malformed lines.\n\nThus algorithm:\n- Initialize trajectory = []\n- i = 0\n- expected_next_type = None (or state machine)\n- For each line in lines:\n    - Try to split at first \": \". If not two parts or type not in {\"Thought\",\"Action\",\"Observation\",\"Final\"}, then set error_line = i, valid = false, break. (But continue to build trajectory? If we break, we don't process further lines. But the trajectory should include all processed lines? The output schema requires trajectory, and we must return a result. We can build trajectory of processed lines up to error (including error line). Then return. But need to decide. Since error_line indicates the line that caused error, trajectory should include that line as well? Yes, because we parsed it. So we can still add it to trajectory. Then break loop.\n    - Append {\"type\": type, \"content\": content} to trajectory.\n    - Check sequence using state machine:\n\nState machine: \n- states: start, after_thought, after_action, after_observation, after_final (terminal)\n- transitions:\n    - start: only \"Thought\" allowed.\n    - after_thought: allowed next types: \"Action\", \"Final\" (if not last, but can be last). If \"Final\" -> go to after_final (no more). If \"Action\" -> go to after_action.\n    - after_action: only \"Observation\" allowed.\n    - after_observation: only \"Thought\" allowed.\n    - after_final: no more lines allowed; if any line after final, error.\n\nAlso, first line must be \"Thought\". So at i=0, if not \"Thought\", error_line=0.\n\nWe also need to ensure that after \"Final\", there are no more lines. So after processing \"Final\", if there are more lines, error at that next line.\n\nWe'll iterate and check transitions. If transition invalid, set error_line to current line index, valid=false, break.\n\nAt end of loop (if no errors), valid=true, error_line=null.\n\nEdge cases: empty lines list? Not given, but if empty, then no first Thought -> invalid? Could consider error_line=0? But not sure. We'll assume non-empty.\n\nAlso, the valid case b-000: after Thought (index3) we have Final (index4) -> after_thought allowed Final. That's fine.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use 0-based index for error_line.\n- The trajectory should include all lines processed (including invalid line) before breaking? Or include all lines? Since we break on first error, we will have trajectory up to and including the error line. That's fine.\n\nReturn dict with valid, error_line, trajectory.\n\nNow, the Python source must be inside a JSON string under key \"implementation\". The function signature: solve(case) where case is the payload object (dictionary). It has key \"lines\". Return a JSON-serializable object.\n\nWe need to output only the JSON envelope: {\"implementation\": \"<Python code as string>\"}. The code must be valid Python.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nNote: The task scaffold says \"do not use randomness, clocks, etc.\" Fine.\n\nImplementation code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case[\"lines\"]\n    trajectory = []\n    state = \"start\"  # start, after_thought, after_action, after_observation, after_final\n    error_line = None\n    valid = True\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n        # parse line\n        parts = line.split(\": \", 1)\n        if len(parts) != 2:\n            # invalid format\n            error_line = i\n            valid = False\n            # still add to trajectory? We'll add with type=line? but we need type string. Let's set type as line and content \"\"? Better to conform to schema: type and content are strings. We can set type=line and content=\"\" but that may not match expected. Since fixtures have proper format, this case won't happen. We'll handle by setting type=\"\" and content=line? Not ideal. But let's assume input is always formatted. However, to be safe, we can treat as error and still add an object with type=line, content=\"\"? The schema doesn't specify content for error lines. But we'll add anyway.\n            trajectory.append({\"type\": \"Unknown\", \"content\": line})  # or something\n            break\n        typ, content = parts\n        if typ not in (\"Thought\",\"Action\",\"Observation\",\"Final\"):\n            error_line = i\n            valid = False\n            trajectory.append({\"type\": typ, \"content\": content})  # still add\n            break\n        trajectory.append({\"type\": typ, \"content\": content})\n        # check state transition\n        if state == \"start\":\n            if typ != \"Thought\":\n                error_line = i\n                valid = False\n                break\n            state = \"after_thought\"\n        elif state == \"after_thought\":\n            if typ == \"Action\":\n                state = \"after_action\"\n            elif typ == \"Final\":\n                state = \"after_final\"\n            else:\n                # unexpected type: like Thought or Observation\n                error_line = i\n                valid = False\n                break\n        elif state == \"after_action\":\n            if typ != \"Observation\":\n                error_line = i\n                valid = False\n                break\n            state = \"after_observation\"\n        elif state == \"after_observation\":\n            if typ != \"Thought\":\n                error_line = i\n                valid = False\n                break\n            state = \"after_thought\"\n        elif state == \"after_final\":\n            # no more steps allowed\n            error_line = i\n            valid = False\n            break\n    else:\n        # loop completed without break; check that final state is not \"start\" (empty) or \"after_action\" (incomplete)\n        # valid sequences end with after_final (if last was Final) or after_thought? Actually after Final we end. But could it end with after_thought? In valid examples, after thought we have Final. But could the trajectory end without Final? e.g., just \"Thought\"? That would be incomplete. The problem likely expects that the trajectory must end with \"Final\". Let's check fixtures: valid case ends with Final. So we enforce that the final step must be \"Final\" and state after_final. So if state != \"after_final\" after processing all lines, error at last line? Or set error_line to len(lines)-1? Actually no specific line is invalid, but the sequence is incomplete. We can set error_line to the index of the last line? The last line is valid as a standalone, but the sequence is missing Final. Possibly the expected validation requires that the trajectory ends with Final. So we need to check that the last line is \"Final\". If not, invalid. So after loop, if state != \"after_final\": set valid=False, error_line = len(lines)-1 (or maybe null? But error_line should be integer). The instruction: error_line integer or null. For incomplete sequence, we can set error_line to the index of the last line? But the problem examples don't cover this. The fixtures all have Final. So we can assume all provided tests have Final. But to be safe, we can implement: if not valid: already set. If valid and state != \"after_final\": set valid=False, error_line = len(lines)-1. Or we could set error_line = null and valid=False? The schema says error_line is required and must be integer or null, so we must provide an integer if invalid. So we set to last index.\n        if valid and state != \"after_final\":\n            valid = False\n            error_line = len(lines) - 1  # last line is the problem? Actually the last line is not Final, so error at that line.\n        else:\n            # valid\n            error_line = None\n\n    return {\"valid\": valid, \"error_line\": error_line, \"trajectory\": trajectory}\n\nBut note: In the loop, if we break due to error, we still have trajectory including error line. That's fine. The final state might not be set after break. So after loop, we should not override error_line if already set. So we check if not valid (already false) then keep. Else, check final state.\n\nAlso, we need to ensure we don't mutate case - we are not modifying.\n\nEdge: The trajectory should include all lines processed, even if error. So we add the line that caused error before breaking? Yes, we add then break. That means the erroneous line is included. Acceptable.\n\nNow we need to output as JSON string with escaped quotes etc.\n\nWe'll produce the envelope.\n\nBut careful: The output schema for solve(case) must match the structural schema exactly. That includes \"error_line\" (integer or null), \"trajectory\" (array of objects with \"content\" and \"type\"), \"valid\" (boolean). We are returning that.\n\nNow, the implementation code must be a string value for the \"implementation\" key. So we need to write the code as a string with proper escaping.\n\nWe'll generate the JSON output. Since the assistant must respond with the JSON object directly, we produce it.\n\nLet's write the code with careful indentation. Use 4 spaces.\n\nWe must avoid any external imports.\n\nWe'll also handle potential ValueError if split fails, but we used if len(parts)!=2.\n\nAlso, note: The line might have multiple colons, e.g., \"Thought: inspect: more\". Our split with maxsplit=1 will split only on first \": \", giving type=\"Thought\", content=\"inspect: more\". That's fine.\n\nNow, produce final answer.\n\nBut wait: The task description says \"The attached public fixtures define input shapes only.\" That means the provided fixtures are examples, but the actual test cases may have different lines. Our solution should generalize.\n\nWe'll implement the state machine as described.\n\nAlso, consider that after \"Final\", there should be no more lines. If there are, we set error_line to that line index (the line after final). Our loop handles that: after processing Final, state becomes after_final; next iteration, it will detect and set error.\n\nNow, after loop, we check if trajectory empty? Then error at line0? But we handle start.\n\nNow, code.\n\nPotential issue: In the loop, after parsing and adding to trajectory, we check state transition. But if state transition fails, we set error_line and break. That means we added the line to trajectory before recognizing error. That's fine.\n\nBut in case of error due to format (split failure), we add an object with type \"Unknown\" and content line, but the type is not in allowed types. That might be fine. However, if the type is not one of the four but format is correct, we still add it. That's okay.\n\nNow, double-check the required keys: \"type\" and \"content\" in trajectory items. We have them.\n\nNow, produce the final JSON.\n\nWe'll write the Python code as a multi-line string. Then embed it in a JSON object.\n\nWe need to escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". In Python, we can use json.dumps for the entire response to ensure proper escaping. But the instruction says: \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So we need to manually escape or use json.dumps. Since the entire response is a JSON object, we can programmatically build it with json.dumps in the assistant's mind. But as an AI, we can output the JSON directly with proper escaping. We'll do it manually.\n\nWrite the Python code as a single string with newlines represented as \\n in JSON. We'll use triple quotes? Actually in JSON string, we need to escape double quotes and backslashes. We'll write the code and then escape.\n\nBetter to use a raw idea: We'll construct a string in our mind and output.\n\nI'll write the code compactly to avoid many newlines, but it's okay.\n\nImplementation code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    lines = case['lines']\n    trajectory = []\n    state = 'start'\n    valid = True\n    error_line = None\n    for i, line in enumerate(lines):\n        parts = line.split(': ', 1)\n        if len(parts) != 2:\n            trajectory.append({'type': 'Unknown', 'content': line})\n            error_line = i\n            valid = False\n            break\n        typ, content = parts\n        if typ not in ('Thought','Action','Observation','Final'):\n            trajectory.append({'type': typ, 'content': content})\n            error_line = i\n            valid = False\n            break\n        trajectory.append({'type': typ, 'content': content})\n        if state == 'start':\n            if typ != 'Thought':\n                error_line = i\n                valid = False\n                break\n            state = 'after_thought'\n        elif state == 'after_thought':\n            if typ == 'Action':\n                state = 'after_action'\n            elif typ == 'Final':\n                state = 'after_final'\n            else:\n                error_line = i\n                valid = False\n                break\n        elif state == 'after_action':\n            if typ != 'Observation':\n                error_line = i\n                valid = False\n                break\n            state = 'after_observation'\n        elif state == 'after_observation':\n            if typ != 'Thought':\n                error_line = i\n                valid = False\n                break\n            state = 'after_thought'\n        elif state == 'after_final':\n            error_line = i\n            valid = False\n            break\n    else:\n        if valid and state != 'after_final':\n            valid = False\n            error_line = len(lines) - 1\n        else:\n            error_line = None\n    return {'valid': valid, 'error_line': error_line, 'trajectory': trajectory}\n\nNow, we need to ensure it returns a JSON-serializable object. Yes.\n\nNow, put this code into a JSON string. We'l